//! Zeebra itself as an [`ExternalSolver`], so the competitive board has something to compare to.
//!
//! # Why Zeebra goes through the same contract as the subprocesses
//!
//! The board's ratios are only meaningful if both operands were measured the same way. Putting
//! Zeebra behind the same trait as msolve and Singular means one code path decides what a `Task`
//! is, what `Limits` mean, which outcomes are timing evidence, and where a ratio may be computed —
//! so the in-crate row cannot acquire a privilege the subprocess rows lack. It also means the
//! in-crate row is subject to the same refusals: a task Zeebra does not offer comes back
//! [`Unsupported`], not silently absent.
//!
//! # Which tasks this serves, and why each refusal is its own
//!
//! Basis, lex basis, conversion and the **univariate eliminant**. The eliminant is served because
//! it is a *projection of an answer already computed* — `I ∩ k[x_n]` is generated by the one
//! element of a reduced lex basis involving no other variable — so it carries no new algorithm and
//! no strategy choice. It used to be refused alongside the two solution tasks and the witness,
//! under one comment saying "no comparand offers the task at all", and that was **false for the
//! eliminant**: msolve serves it below `MAX_CHARACTERISTIC_ELIMINANT` and Singular has a dedicated
//! arm, which made it the only task the board defines with two external engines and no in-crate
//! operand (#465). Each remaining refusal now carries the reason that applies to it.
//!
//! Note the **route differs across engines and that is the comparison, not a defect**: Zeebra
//! reaches the eliminant by grevlex F4 then FGLM, Singular by a lex `std`, msolve by its rational
//! parametrization. The `stages` a row carries make Zeebra's route visible. What has to agree is
//! the object — the eliminant's degree is compared as an invariant, and for a radical ideal in
//! shape position it coincides with the quotient dimension, which is a **precondition rather than
//! a given** and so is reported beside it rather than asserted from it.
//!
//! # What is **not** equivalent, and is recorded rather than hidden
//!
//! Zeebra runs **in this process**, so its peak RSS is [`RssBasis::SelfHighWater`] where an
//! external tool's is [`RssBasis::DirectChildHighWater`]. Those are the same *quantity* — a
//! kernel-maintained `VmHWM` for one process — and differ only in attribution: a subprocess
//! figure covers one solve because the supervisor spawns one, while this process may have run
//! several rows and whatever built their inputs. So the figure is reported **only when the caller
//! declares that this process is doing one row and nothing else**
//! ([`ZeebraSolver::measuring_one_row`]), and is withheld otherwise. Withheld, not approximated:
//! `docs/research/external-comparison-protocol.md` §4 is the reasoning, and a ceiling over several
//! rows presented as one row's footprint is exactly the confusion it exists to stop.
//!
//! Zeebra also gets no process-startup charge, where Julia's and msolve's wall times include
//! theirs. That favours Zeebra, it cannot be removed without running Zeebra as a subprocess too,
//! and it is immaterial at the sizes where the gaps are interesting and material on the
//! millisecond rungs — which is why the report states it beside the small-instance rows.
//!
//! # Dimension and field dispatch
//!
//! `f4` and `fglm_to_lex` are const-generic in the variable count and generic in the field, so a
//! text instance has to be dispatched to a compiled instantiation. [`run`](ZeebraSolver::run)
//! matches on the characteristic and then on the variable count, and the set of pairs it compiles
//! is exactly what the corpus ladder needs. A pair nobody wrote down is
//! [`Unsupported::Representation`] naming itself as an **adapter** limit rather than an engine
//! one — Zeebra solves plenty of shapes this file does not instantiate, and a board row must not
//! imply otherwise.

use std::time::{Duration, Instant};

use super::{
    contract::{
        Answer, ExternalSolver, Instance, Invariants, LimitStop, Limits, Measurement, RunFailure,
        RunReport, RunStatus, Support, Task, ToolVersion, Unsupported,
    },
    corpus::{
        ALTERNATE_CHARACTERISTIC, KOALABEAR_CHARACTERISTIC, REFERENCE_CHARACTERISTIC,
        SMALL_PRIME_CHARACTERISTIC, WIDE_64_CHARACTERISTIC,
    },
    process::{CpuBasis, RssBasis},
};
/// The quotient dimension this adapter will enumerate to **report an invariant**.
///
/// Deliberately far above `FglmOptions`'s conversion default of 4 096. A dimension is obtained by
/// enumerating standard monomials, which `FglmOptions`'s own documentation calls cheap at any such
/// dimension — it is the operator and span entry budgets that bound memory, and an invariant needs
/// neither. Tying the two together made a *basis* row's verifiability hostage to a *conversion*
/// budget, which cost the `mq-planted-14` basis row its dimension.
const INVARIANT_DIMENSION_CEILING: usize = 1 << 20;

use crate::{
    fields::{PrimeFieldElement64, Zp32003},
    grobner::{
        differential::polynomial_to_string,
        f4_reported,
        fglm::{classify_ideal, fglm_to_lex_with_options, FglmOptions, QuotientBasis},
        F4Error, F4Options, IdealClass, InterreductionRoute,
    },
    util::peak_resident_bytes,
    FiniteField, MonomialOrder, MultivariatePolynomial,
};

/// Zeebra's own engine, behind the competitive contract.
#[derive(Clone, Debug)]
pub struct ZeebraSolver {
    f4_options: F4Options,
    fglm_options: FglmOptions,
    measuring_one_row: bool,
}

impl Default for ZeebraSolver {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self::new()
    }
}

impl ZeebraSolver {
    /// A solver at the default F4 and FGLM options, with no memory figure reported.
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Self {
            f4_options: F4Options::default(),
            fglm_options: FglmOptions::default(),
            measuring_one_row: false,
        }
    }

    /// Set the F4 options, including `max_wall_time`, which is how [`Limits::wall`] is enforced.
    pub fn with_f4_options(mut self, options: F4Options) -> Self {
        self.f4_options = options;
        self
    }

    /// Set the FGLM options.
    pub fn with_fglm_options(mut self, options: FglmOptions) -> Self {
        self.fglm_options = options;
        self
    }

    /// Declare that this process runs **one row and nothing else**, so its peak RSS is that row's.
    ///
    /// Only then is a memory figure reported. `VmHWM` is process-wide, so in a process that ran
    /// several rows the figure is a ceiling over all of them and over whatever built their inputs
    /// — a ceiling, not a per-row figure. Here
    /// the sweep harness runs one process per measured row and sets this; a multi-row exploratory
    /// run does not, and its rows carry no memory figure at all.
    pub fn measuring_one_row(mut self) -> Self {
        self.measuring_one_row = true;
        self
    }

    /// The peak RSS to report, which is `None` unless this process is doing one row.
    fn memory(&self) -> (Option<u64>, RssBasis) {
        if !self.measuring_one_row {
            return (None, RssBasis::Unavailable);
        }
        match peak_resident_bytes() {
            Some(bytes) => (Some(bytes), RssBasis::SelfHighWater),
            None => (None, RssBasis::Unavailable),
        }
    }
}

/// Which (characteristic, variable count) pairs this adapter compiles.
///
/// Exactly the corpus ladder's, listed so the monomorphization cost is a deliberate choice rather
/// than whatever a generic call site happened to need. Each field appears with only the dimensions
/// its own families use.
fn compiled_dimensions(characteristic: u64) -> &'static [usize] {
    match characteristic {
        // Katsura 4–11, Cyclic 4–10, random and planted MQ at 6, 10, 14.
        //
        // The same list at every prime the corpus generates these families over
        // (`corpus::GENERATED_CHARACTERISTICS`). None of them is optional for the **eliminant**,
        // and the reference prime is the one that does not serve it: msolve's bound on that task
        // is `1515531529`, so a Zeebra-against-msolve eliminant comparison exists only below it.
        // Compiling the reference prime alone left the one task with two external engines still
        // unable to produce a Zeebra operand — the #465 defect from the other direction.
        //
        // Widths 8–11 are #424's upper rungs, and they are the expensive half of this list: each
        // (field, width) pair monomorphizes F4 *and* FGLM, so every width added here is another
        // copy of both at every prime. They are here rather than omitted because a board row
        // saying `Unsupported::Representation` on a rung the corpus publishes would report an
        // adapter gap as a Zeebra limit, which is the one confusion this file exists to prevent.
        REFERENCE_CHARACTERISTIC | ALTERNATE_CHARACTERISTIC => &[4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 14],
        // The same rungs at the corpus's small-prime identity. Over `Zp32003`, a 32-bit
        // Montgomery field — so a small-prime row measures the narrow arithmetic regime rather
        // than the 64-bit path at a small modulus.
        SMALL_PRIME_CHARACTERISTIC => &[4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 14],
        // KoalaBear and 2^64 - 353 at the widths their callers' systems have been measured at.
        // A caller needing another width adds it here; see #668.
        KOALABEAR_CHARACTERISTIC => &[5, 7, 9],
        WIDE_64_CHARACTERISTIC => &[2, 4],
        _ => &[],
    }
}

/// What one solve produced, before it becomes a [`RunReport`].
struct Solved {
    status: RunStatus,
    wall: Duration,
    stages: Vec<(String, Duration)>,
}

/// The element of a reduced lex basis that generates `I ∩ k[x_variable]`, if there is one.
///
/// **A projection, not a computation.** For a zero-dimensional ideal the elimination ideal in the
/// last variable is nonzero, and a reduced lex Gröbner basis contains exactly one element
/// involving no other variable — that element generates it. So serving
/// [`Task::UnivariateEliminant`] off an already-computed lex basis adds no algorithm and no
/// strategy choice, which is the question #465 had to settle before un-refusing the task.
///
/// Returns `None` when no such element exists, which for a zero-dimensional ideal means a defect
/// rather than a capability gap. Scans every term rather than only the leading monomial: a
/// generator whose leading monomial is a power of `x_variable` may still carry lower terms in
/// other variables, and reporting one of those as an eliminant would be wrong in a way that looks
/// right.
fn univariate_eliminant<const N: usize, F: FiniteField>(
    lex: &[MultivariatePolynomial<N, F>],
    variable: usize,
) -> Option<&MultivariatePolynomial<N, F>> {
    lex.iter().find(|polynomial| {
        !polynomial.is_zero()
            && polynomial.terms().keys().all(|monomial| {
                monomial
                    .exponents()
                    .iter()
                    .enumerate()
                    .all(|(index, exponent)| index == variable || *exponent == 0)
            })
    })
}

/// Run `task` on a parsed system at a compiled dimension and field.
///
/// Separate from [`ExternalSolver::run`] because it is the only part that is generic: everything
/// around it — the capability check, the measurement, the report — is dimension-agnostic and must
/// not be monomorphized with it.
fn solve<const N: usize, F: FiniteField>(
    generators: &[MultivariatePolynomial<N, F>],
    names: &[&str],
    task: Task,
    order: MonomialOrder,
    f4_options: &F4Options,
    fglm_options: &FglmOptions,
) -> Solved {
    let mut stages: Vec<(String, Duration)> = Vec::new();
    let started = Instant::now();

    let at = Instant::now();
    let (result, statistics) = f4_reported(generators, order, f4_options);
    let basis_time = at.elapsed();
    stages.push(("basis".to_string(), basis_time));

    let basis = match result {
        Ok(basis) => basis,
        Err(error) => {
            // A resource or time stop is **not** a failure of the engine, and the contract has a
            // separate outcome for it. `limit_name` is the stable key; `max_wall_time` is the one
            // that is a time stop, and the harness sets it from `Limits::wall`.
            let status = match error {
                // Both time stops, and both must land here rather than in the `other` arm below:
                // that arm reports a `ToolCap`, so an unlisted time stop would be recorded as a
                // capability limit of the engine instead of as the wall clock it is. The variants
                // differ only in which stage noticed the budget — the batch loop or final
                // interreduction (#395) — which is not a distinction the contract draws.
                F4Error::TimeBudgetExceeded {
                    elapsed_millis,
                    budget_millis,
                    ..
                }
                | F4Error::InterreductionTimeBudgetExceeded {
                    elapsed_millis,
                    budget_millis,
                    ..
                } => RunStatus::StoppedByLimit(LimitStop::WallClock {
                    limit: Duration::from_millis(budget_millis),
                    elapsed: Duration::from_millis(elapsed_millis),
                }),
                F4Error::InvalidOptions { reason } => RunStatus::Failed(RunFailure::Reported {
                    message: reason.to_string(),
                }),
                other => RunStatus::StoppedByLimit(LimitStop::ToolCap {
                    detail: format!("{} ({})", other, other.limit_name()),
                }),
            };
            return Solved {
                status,
                wall: started.elapsed(),
                stages,
            };
        }
    };

    // The invariants a cross-library check compares, extracted from the basis Zeebra returned.
    // Leading monomials are the initial ideal, which is what two correct reduced bases share.
    let leading: Vec<String> = basis
        .iter()
        .map(|polynomial| polynomial.leading_monomial(order))
        .map(|monomial| crate::grobner::differential::monomial_to_string(&monomial, names))
        .collect();
    let mut invariants = Invariants::none().with_leading_monomials(leading);

    let basis_text: Vec<String> = basis
        .iter()
        .map(|polynomial| polynomial_to_string(polynomial, names, order))
        .collect();

    match task {
        Task::ReducedGrobnerBasis | Task::LexBasis => {
            // The quotient dimension is an invariant worth reporting whenever it is finite, and
            // `classify_ideal` is what says whether it is. A positive-dimensional ideal reports
            // none rather than zero.
            if classify_ideal(&basis, order) == IdealClass::ZeroDimensional {
                let at = Instant::now();
                // `QuotientBasis::new` and **not** a full conversion. Enumerating the standard
                // monomials is all a dimension needs; running `fglm_to_lex` for it meant a
                // *basis* row's verifiability depended on an unrelated FGLM budget, and on
                // `mq-planted-14` it did: the basis finished in 0.28 ms, the 16 384-dimensional
                // enumeration tripped the 4 096 operator cap, and the row went out with **no
                // dimension at all** — against a Singular row that reported 16 384. Nothing
                // shared is not agreement, so that row was silently unverified while looking
                // like one of a set that agreed.
                //
                // `INVARIANT_DIMENSION_CEILING` is its own bound, far above the conversion
                // default, because `FglmOptions`'s own documentation says the enumeration is
                // cheap at any such dimension and it is the entry budgets that bound memory.
                match QuotientBasis::new(&basis, order, INVARIANT_DIMENSION_CEILING) {
                    Ok(quotient) => {
                        invariants = invariants.with_quotient_dimension(quotient.dimension());
                    }
                    Err(error) => {
                        // Recorded as a stage rather than swallowed: a row whose invariant could
                        // not be computed must say so, or it reads as a row nobody checked.
                        stages.push((
                            format!("quotient-dimension UNAVAILABLE: {}", error),
                            at.elapsed(),
                        ));
                    }
                }
                // Charged as its own stage: it is work done to *report an invariant*, not part of
                // the task, and leaving it in the basis time would make this row slower than the
                // same engine asked the same question without the cross-check.
                stages.push((
                    "quotient-dimension (for the invariant)".to_string(),
                    at.elapsed(),
                ));
            }
            Solved {
                status: RunStatus::Complete(
                    Answer::from_invariants(invariants).with_basis(basis_text),
                ),
                // Excludes the invariant stage above, so the published figure is the task's cost.
                wall: basis_time,
                stages,
            }
        }
        Task::LexBasisByConversion | Task::UnivariateEliminant => {
            if classify_ideal(&basis, order) != IdealClass::ZeroDimensional {
                return Solved {
                    status: RunStatus::Unsupported(Unsupported::PositiveDimensional { task }),
                    wall: started.elapsed(),
                    stages,
                };
            }
            let at = Instant::now();
            let converted = fglm_to_lex_with_options(&basis, order, fglm_options);
            let conversion_time = at.elapsed();
            stages.push(("conversion".to_string(), conversion_time));

            match converted {
                Ok((lex, statistics)) => {
                    // The lex basis's own leading monomials, which is what another engine asked
                    // for the same task would report. Not the source basis's.
                    let lex_leading: Vec<String> = lex
                        .iter()
                        .map(|p| p.leading_monomial(MonomialOrder::Lexicographic))
                        .map(|m| crate::grobner::differential::monomial_to_string(&m, names))
                        .collect();
                    let timings = statistics.timings;
                    stages.push(("conversion: dependence".to_string(), timings.dependence));
                    stages.push((
                        "conversion: border".to_string(),
                        timings.normal_forms + timings.border_symbolic,
                    ));
                    let mut invariants = Invariants::none()
                        .with_leading_monomials(lex_leading)
                        .with_quotient_dimension(statistics.quotient_dimension);
                    let mut answer_eliminant = None;

                    if task == Task::UnivariateEliminant {
                        // The eliminant is a **projection of the answer already computed**, not a
                        // second algorithm: `I ∩ k[x_n]` is generated by the one element of a
                        // reduced lex basis that involves no variable but the last, and for a
                        // zero-dimensional ideal that element exists. So this task carries no
                        // new strategy choice — which is what separates it from the solution
                        // tasks, whose refusal is about a root-finding choice being reported as
                        // a bare timing.
                        match univariate_eliminant(&lex, N - 1) {
                            Some(eliminant) => {
                                // Total degree, which for a polynomial in one variable is its
                                // degree in that variable.
                                invariants =
                                    invariants.with_eliminant_degree(eliminant.degree() as usize);
                                answer_eliminant = Some(polynomial_to_string(
                                    eliminant,
                                    names,
                                    MonomialOrder::Lexicographic,
                                ));
                            }
                            // Unreachable for a zero-dimensional ideal, and so a defect rather
                            // than a capability gap if it happens: `Failed` keeps it out of the
                            // timing evidence instead of publishing a row against a blank.
                            None => {
                                return Solved {
                                    status: RunStatus::Failed(RunFailure::Reported {
                                        message: format!(
                                            "the lex basis has no element univariate in {}, which \
                                             a zero-dimensional ideal must have",
                                            names.last().copied().unwrap_or("?")
                                        ),
                                    }),
                                    wall: started.elapsed(),
                                    stages,
                                };
                            }
                        }
                    }

                    let mut answer = Answer::from_invariants(invariants).with_basis(
                        lex.iter()
                            .map(|p| polynomial_to_string(p, names, MonomialOrder::Lexicographic))
                            .collect(),
                    );
                    if let Some(eliminant) = answer_eliminant {
                        answer = answer.with_eliminant(eliminant);
                    }
                    Solved {
                        status: RunStatus::Complete(answer),
                        wall: basis_time + conversion_time,
                        stages,
                    }
                }
                // **A declared budget is a limit stop, not a failure.** Recording
                // `QuotientDimensionExceeded` as `Failed` put a resource cap in the bucket
                // reserved for an engine that broke — the exact confusion this contract exists
                // to prevent, and it showed up on the first corpus sweep that reached a rung
                // over the cap. The two genuine failures are kept apart: a positive-dimensional
                // ideal is a fact about the ideal, and a source that was not a Gröbner basis is
                // a caller error.
                Err(error) => {
                    use crate::grobner::FglmError::*;
                    let status = match error {
                        OperatorStorageExceeded { .. }
                        | OperatorBytesExceeded { .. }
                        | SpanStorageExceeded { .. }
                        | SpanBytesExceeded { .. }
                        | BorderBatchExceeded { .. }
                        | QuotientDimensionExceeded { .. } => {
                            RunStatus::StoppedByLimit(LimitStop::ToolCap {
                                detail: error.to_string(),
                            })
                        }
                        // Not a budget: no limit would make this possible, so it is a statement
                        // about the input rather than about the resources.
                        PositiveDimensional => {
                            RunStatus::Unsupported(Unsupported::PositiveDimensional { task })
                        }
                        other => RunStatus::Failed(RunFailure::Reported {
                            message: other.to_string(),
                        }),
                    };
                    Solved {
                        status,
                        wall: started.elapsed(),
                        stages,
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        // `statistics` is read here only so that a reader can see the basis stage was genuinely
        // measured rather than reported from an unrelated run.
        _ => Solved {
            status: RunStatus::Unsupported(Unsupported::Task { task }),
            wall: started.elapsed(),
            stages: {
                let mut stages = stages;
                stages.push((format!("batches {}", statistics.batches), Duration::ZERO));
                stages
            },
        },
    }
}

/// Parse `instance`'s text at a compiled dimension and solve it.
///
/// A macro because the dimension is a const generic: one arm per compiled `N`, and the field type
/// is the caller's. Returns `None` for a dimension this field does not compile.
macro_rules! dispatch {
    ($field:ty, $instance:expr, $task:expr, $f4:expr, $fglm:expr, $($n:literal),+ $(,)?) => {{
        let names: Vec<&str> = $instance.variables.iter().map(String::as_str).collect();
        match $instance.variables.len() {
            $(
                $n => {
                    let mut compiled = [""; $n];
                    compiled.copy_from_slice(&names[..$n]);
                    let mut generators: Vec<MultivariatePolynomial<$n, $field>> = Vec::new();
                    for text in &$instance.system {
                        match MultivariatePolynomial::parse(text, compiled) {
                            Ok(polynomial) => generators.push(polynomial),
                            // A generator this crate's own renderer produced and its own parser
                            // refuses is a bug, not a capability gap, and says so.
                            Err(e) => {
                                return Err(RunFailure::Unparsable {
                                    marker: "generator".to_string(),
                                    value: format!("{}: {}", text, e),
                                })
                            }
                        }
                    }
                    Ok(solve(&generators, &compiled, $task, $instance.order, $f4, $fglm))
                }
            )+
            _ => Err(RunFailure::Incomplete {
                detail: "no compiled dimension, which `supports` should have refused".to_string(),
            }),
        }
    }};
}

impl ZeebraSolver {
    /// Parse and solve, dispatching on the characteristic and then the variable count.
    fn dispatch(&self, instance: &Instance, task: Task) -> Result<Solved, RunFailure> {
        match instance.field.characteristic {
            SMALL_PRIME_CHARACTERISTIC => dispatch!(
                Zp32003,
                instance,
                task,
                &self.f4_options,
                &self.fglm_options,
                4,
                5,
                6,
                7,
                8,
                9,
                10,
                11,
                14
            ),
            ALTERNATE_CHARACTERISTIC => dispatch!(
                PrimeFieldElement64<ALTERNATE_CHARACTERISTIC>,
                instance,
                task,
                &self.f4_options,
                &self.fglm_options,
                4,
                5,
                6,
                7,
                8,
                9,
                10,
                11,
                14
            ),
            REFERENCE_CHARACTERISTIC => dispatch!(
                PrimeFieldElement64<REFERENCE_CHARACTERISTIC>,
                instance,
                task,
                &self.f4_options,
                &self.fglm_options,
                4,
                5,
                6,
                7,
                8,
                9,
                10,
                11,
                14
            ),
            KOALABEAR_CHARACTERISTIC => dispatch!(
                PrimeFieldElement64<KOALABEAR_CHARACTERISTIC>,
                instance,
                task,
                &self.f4_options,
                &self.fglm_options,
                5,
                7,
                9
            ),
            WIDE_64_CHARACTERISTIC => dispatch!(
                PrimeFieldElement64<WIDE_64_CHARACTERISTIC>,
                instance,
                task,
                &self.f4_options,
                &self.fglm_options,
                2,
                4
            ),
            _ => Err(RunFailure::Incomplete {
                detail: "no compiled field, which `supports` should have refused".to_string(),
            }),
        }
    }
}

impl ExternalSolver for ZeebraSolver {
    fn id(&self) -> &str {
        "zeebra"
    }

    fn probe_version(&mut self) -> Result<ToolVersion, RunFailure> {
        // No subprocess: the version is this build's, and the commit is what a record needs to be
        // reproducible. `CARGO_PKG_VERSION` is a compile-time constant, so there is nothing to
        // fail — but the signature keeps the `Result` so that no caller has to special-case
        // Zeebra, which is the whole point of putting it behind the contract.
        //
        // The build string also names the interreduction route the options select. The commit does
        // not determine it — `InterreductionRoute` is a caller's option, so two rows taken at one
        // commit on either side of it agree on every field a ratio is checked against
        // (`problem_digest`, `task`, `limits`, `machine`, `build`) while measuring different
        // computations. #507: a board row that cannot say which route produced it is not a board
        // row the default can be decided on.
        //
        // The *option* is reported rather than the route it resolves to, because `ByOrder` resolves
        // per order and the row already carries its order. Resolving it here would mean a version
        // string that can disagree with the options the caller set.
        let route = match self.f4_options.interreduction {
            InterreductionRoute::ByOrder => "by-order",
            InterreductionRoute::Division => "division",
            InterreductionRoute::Matrix => "matrix",
        };
        // `reduce_batch_rows` is named for the same reason and is not independent of the route:
        // with batches left reduced the autoreduce matrix falls to a few hundred columns, so the
        // route's cost is a different quantity. A row naming one flag and not the other would be a
        // row whose configuration cannot be reconstructed.
        Ok(
            ToolVersion::new("zeebra", env!("CARGO_PKG_VERSION")).with_build(format!(
                "in-process, same build as the harness; interreduction={}, batch-rows={}",
                route,
                if self.f4_options.reduce_batch_rows {
                    "reduced"
                } else {
                    "echelon"
                }
            )),
        )
    }

    fn supports(&self, instance: &Instance, task: Task) -> Support {
        // **No weighted-order refusal here, unlike the four subprocess adapters.** Zeebra's own
        // engine computes under a weighted order (#306), and the two reasons the board could not
        // pose one are both gone: #624 verified a comparand's weighted spelling, and #625 gave the
        // order an injective tag that round-trips, so an instance carrying one has a textual
        // contract and a digest of its own. `order_is_unsupported_externally` is now about the
        // external tools, and this adapter is not one.
        //
        // A weighted row is therefore a one-engine row until a subprocess adapter follows — which
        // is the same state the univariate eliminant was in before msolve joined it, and the board
        // records a one-operand column honestly rather than refusing to hold it.
        match task {
            Task::ReducedGrobnerBasis
            | Task::LexBasis
            | Task::LexBasisByConversion
            | Task::UnivariateEliminant => {}
            // Zeebra has `solve_one`, but routing it through this adapter would mean reporting a
            // root-finding strategy choice as a bare timing, and **no comparand offers either
            // solution task at all** (the protocol §3): msolve's prime-field output is a
            // parametrization and not an enumeration (`msolve.rs` §3.5), and Singular's `solve`
            // is numerical. A column with one operand is not a comparison.
            Task::OneFiniteFieldSolution | Task::AllFiniteFieldSolutions => {
                return Support::No(Unsupported::Task { task });
            }
        }
        if task == Task::LexBasis && instance.order != MonomialOrder::Lexicographic {
            return Support::No(Unsupported::Order {
                order: instance.order,
            });
        }
        if !instance.field.is_prime_field() {
            return Support::No(Unsupported::ExtensionField {
                characteristic: instance.field.characteristic,
                extension_degree: instance.field.extension_degree,
            });
        }

        let dimensions = compiled_dimensions(instance.field.characteristic);
        if !dimensions.contains(&instance.variables.len()) {
            // **An adapter limit, not an engine limit**, and the wording has to say so: Zeebra
            // solves shapes this file does not instantiate, and a board row reading "unsupported"
            // against Zeebra would otherwise be a false statement about the engine.
            return Support::No(Unsupported::Representation {
                detail: format!(
                    "this adapter compiles GF({}) at {:?} variables, not {} — an adapter \
                     monomorphization limit, not a limit of Zeebra's engine",
                    instance.field.characteristic,
                    dimensions,
                    instance.variables.len()
                ),
            });
        }
        Support::Yes
    }

    fn run(&mut self, instance: &Instance, task: Task, limits: &Limits) -> RunReport {
        let version = self.probe_version().ok();
        let finish = |status: RunStatus, measurement: Measurement| {
            let mut report = RunReport::new("zeebra", instance, task, limits, status, measurement);
            if let Some(version) = version.clone() {
                report = report.with_version(version);
            }
            report.with_command_line(format!(
                "cargo run --release --example competitive_sweep -- --row {} --task {}",
                instance.name,
                task.tag()
            ))
        };

        if let Support::No(reason) = self.supports(instance, task) {
            return finish(RunStatus::Unsupported(reason), Measurement::unmeasured());
        }

        match self.dispatch(instance, task) {
            Ok(solved) => {
                let (peak_rss_bytes, rss_basis) = self.memory();
                finish(
                    solved.status,
                    Measurement {
                        wall: solved.wall,
                        // No per-stage CPU time: this is one thread of this process, so a
                        // `/proc/self/stat` read would charge whatever else the process did.
                        cpu: None,
                        cpu_basis: CpuBasis::Unavailable,
                        peak_rss_bytes,
                        rss_basis,
                        stages: solved.stages,
                    },
                )
            }
            Err(failure) => finish(RunStatus::Failed(failure), Measurement::unmeasured()),
        }
    }
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::{compiled_dimensions, ZeebraSolver};
    use crate::grobner::external::{
        corpus::{
            instance, ALTERNATE_CHARACTERISTIC, REFERENCE_CHARACTERISTIC,
            SMALL_PRIME_CHARACTERISTIC,
        },
        ExternalSolver, Limits, RssBasis, RunStatus, Support, Task, Unsupported,
    };
    use crate::MonomialOrder;
    use std::time::Duration;

    const GREVLEX: MonomialOrder = MonomialOrder::GradedReverseLexicographic;

    fn limits() -> Limits {
        Limits::wall(Duration::from_secs(60))
    }

    /// Zeebra answers a corpus rung with a basis and the invariants a cross-check compares.
    #[test]
    fn test_zeebra_answers_a_corpus_rung_with_invariants() {
        let katsura = instance("katsura-4", REFERENCE_CHARACTERISTIC, GREVLEX).expect("builds");
        let mut solver = ZeebraSolver::new();
        let report = solver.run(&katsura, Task::ReducedGrobnerBasis, &limits());

        let answer = match &report.status {
            RunStatus::Complete(answer) => answer,
            other => panic!("expected Complete, got {:?}", other),
        };
        assert!(answer.basis.as_ref().is_some_and(|b| !b.is_empty()));
        assert!(answer.invariants.leading_monomials.is_some());
        // 8 = 2^(n-1) at n = 3, which is Katsura's published quotient dimension. Asserting the
        // literature's number rather than whatever this run produced is what makes the row a
        // check on Zeebra rather than a recording of it.
        assert_eq!(
            answer.invariants.quotient_dimension,
            Some(8),
            "katsura-4's quotient dimension is 8"
        );
        assert!(report.status.is_timing_evidence());
        assert_eq!(report.solver, "zeebra");
    }

    /// The eliminant task is served, as the projection of a lex basis it is.
    ///
    /// #465's premise: the refusal said "no comparand offers the task at all", and two do — msolve
    /// serves it below `MAX_CHARACTERISTIC_ELIMINANT` and Singular has a dedicated arm. So the
    /// eliminant was the only task the board defines with two external engines and no in-crate
    /// operand, which is a corroboration gain that could not become a row.
    #[test]
    fn test_zeebra_serves_the_eliminant_task_from_the_lex_basis() {
        let katsura = instance("katsura-4", REFERENCE_CHARACTERISTIC, GREVLEX).expect("builds");
        let mut solver = ZeebraSolver::new();
        assert_eq!(
            solver.supports(&katsura, Task::UnivariateEliminant),
            Support::Yes
        );

        let report = solver.run(&katsura, Task::UnivariateEliminant, &limits());
        let answer = match &report.status {
            RunStatus::Complete(answer) => answer,
            other => panic!("expected Complete, got {:?}", other),
        };

        // Katsura-4's published quotient dimension is 8, and the ideal is radical and in shape
        // position, so its eliminant has that degree. Asserting the literature's number is what
        // makes this a check rather than a recording — and it is the invariant msolve and Singular
        // can now be compared against.
        assert_eq!(answer.invariants.quotient_dimension, Some(8));
        assert_eq!(answer.invariants.eliminant_degree, Some(8));

        // Univariate in the last variable of the instance's order, which is what the other two
        // engines report. `x1` must not appear anywhere in it.
        let eliminant = answer.eliminant.as_deref().expect("an eliminant");
        assert!(eliminant.contains("x4"), "{}", eliminant);
        for name in &katsura.variables[..katsura.variables.len() - 1] {
            assert!(
                !eliminant.contains(name.as_str()),
                "{} mentions {}",
                eliminant,
                name
            );
        }
        assert!(report.status.is_timing_evidence());
    }

    /// Each refused task carries its own reason, and the eliminant is no longer among them.
    ///
    /// The grouping #465 is about bundled four tasks behind one comment that held for three of
    /// them. The two solution tasks and the witness stay refused for reasons that are still true.
    #[test]
    fn test_the_remaining_refusals_are_the_three_that_have_a_reason() {
        let katsura = instance("katsura-4", REFERENCE_CHARACTERISTIC, GREVLEX).expect("builds");
        let solver = ZeebraSolver::new();
        for task in [Task::OneFiniteFieldSolution, Task::AllFiniteFieldSolutions] {
            assert_eq!(
                solver.supports(&katsura, task),
                Support::No(Unsupported::Task { task }),
                "{:?} should still be refused",
                task
            );
        }
        for task in [
            Task::ReducedGrobnerBasis,
            Task::LexBasisByConversion,
            Task::UnivariateEliminant,
        ] {
            assert_eq!(solver.supports(&katsura, task), Support::Yes, "{:?}", task);
        }
    }

    /// The eliminant is refused on a positive-dimensional ideal, as a fact about the ideal.
    #[test]
    fn test_the_eliminant_is_unsupported_on_a_positive_dimensional_ideal() {
        use crate::grobner::external::{FieldSpec, Instance};

        // One equation in four variables: a hypersurface, so there is no univariate eliminant.
        let positive = Instance::new(
            "one-equation",
            "synthetic",
            FieldSpec {
                characteristic: REFERENCE_CHARACTERISTIC,
                extension_degree: 1,
                name: format!("GF({REFERENCE_CHARACTERISTIC})"),
                defining_polynomial: None,
            },
            (1..=4).map(|i| format!("x{i}")).collect(),
            vec!["x1*x2+x3".to_string()],
            GREVLEX,
        );
        let mut solver = ZeebraSolver::new();
        match &solver
            .run(&positive, Task::UnivariateEliminant, &limits())
            .status
        {
            RunStatus::Unsupported(Unsupported::PositiveDimensional { task }) => {
                assert_eq!(*task, Task::UnivariateEliminant);
            }
            other => panic!("expected a positive-dimensional refusal, got {:?}", other),
        }
    }

    /// The projection takes the generator that is univariate in *every* term, not in its leader.
    ///
    /// The silent wrong answer this rules out: a generator whose leading monomial is a power of
    /// the last variable can still carry lower terms in other variables, and reporting it as an
    /// eliminant would be wrong in a way that looks right.
    #[test]
    fn test_the_projection_rejects_a_generator_that_is_univariate_only_in_its_leader() {
        use super::univariate_eliminant;
        use crate::fields::PrimeFieldElement64;
        use crate::MultivariatePolynomial;

        type F = PrimeFieldElement64<REFERENCE_CHARACTERISTIC>;
        let names = ["x1", "x2"];
        // `x2^3 + x1` leads on `x2^3` under lex with x1 > x2? No — lex puts x1 first, so this is
        // chosen for its *shape*: it has a term in x1 and so is not an eliminant whatever leads.
        let decoy =
            MultivariatePolynomial::<2, F>::parse("x2^3+x1", names).expect("the decoy parses");
        let genuine =
            MultivariatePolynomial::<2, F>::parse("x2^2+1", names).expect("the eliminant parses");

        assert!(univariate_eliminant(std::slice::from_ref(&decoy), 1).is_none());
        assert_eq!(
            univariate_eliminant(&[decoy, genuine.clone()], 1),
            Some(&genuine)
        );
    }

    /// An interreduction time stop is reported as a wall clock, not as a capability cap.
    ///
    /// The regression this guards is a silent one. The error match has an `other =>` arm that
    /// reports `LimitStop::ToolCap`, so a time stop the arms do not name is recorded as a limit of
    /// the **engine** rather than of the clock — and #369 is about to populate a board from these
    /// records. `limit_name()` returning `max_wall_time` is not enough on its own, because the
    /// match is on the variant and not on the name.
    ///
    /// The squares of four distinct variables have pairwise coprime leading monomials, so the
    /// product criterion takes every pair, the batch loop never runs, and the stop can only come
    /// from final interreduction (#395).
    #[test]
    fn test_an_interreduction_time_stop_is_a_wall_clock_not_a_tool_cap() {
        use crate::grobner::external::{FieldSpec, Instance, LimitStop};
        use crate::grobner::F4Options;

        let coprime = Instance::new(
            "coprime-pair",
            "synthetic",
            FieldSpec {
                characteristic: REFERENCE_CHARACTERISTIC,
                extension_degree: 1,
                name: format!("GF({REFERENCE_CHARACTERISTIC})"),
                defining_polynomial: None,
            },
            // Four variables, because the adapter compiles [4, 5, 6, 7, 10, 14] at this
            // characteristic and a two-variable instance is refused before the engine is reached.
            (1..=4).map(|i| format!("x{i}")).collect(),
            (1..=4).map(|i| format!("x{i}^2")).collect(),
            GREVLEX,
        );

        let mut solver = ZeebraSolver::new().with_f4_options(F4Options {
            max_wall_time: Some(Duration::ZERO),
            ..F4Options::default()
        });
        let report = solver.run(&coprime, Task::ReducedGrobnerBasis, &limits());

        match &report.status {
            RunStatus::StoppedByLimit(LimitStop::WallClock { limit, elapsed }) => {
                assert_eq!(*limit, Duration::ZERO);
                assert!(*elapsed >= *limit);
            }
            RunStatus::StoppedByLimit(LimitStop::ToolCap { detail }) => {
                panic!("an interreduction time stop was filed as a capability cap: {detail}")
            }
            other => panic!("expected a wall-clock stop, got {other:?}"),
        }
    }

    /// Zeebra's quotient dimensions agree with the literature's on three corpus rungs.
    ///
    /// The cross-library check's strength rests on these invariants being right, so they are
    /// checked against **published** values rather than against a previous run of this code:
    /// Katsura-n has `2^(n-1)` solutions and cyclic-5 has 70. An engine agreeing with itself
    /// proves nothing.
    #[test]
    fn test_the_reported_invariants_agree_with_published_values() {
        for (name, published) in [("katsura-4", 8), ("katsura-5", 16), ("cyclic-5", 70)] {
            let built = instance(name, REFERENCE_CHARACTERISTIC, GREVLEX).expect("builds");
            let report = ZeebraSolver::new().run(&built, Task::ReducedGrobnerBasis, &limits());
            let answer = match &report.status {
                RunStatus::Complete(answer) => answer,
                other => panic!("{} gave {:?}", name, other),
            };
            assert_eq!(
                answer.invariants.quotient_dimension,
                Some(published),
                "{}'s quotient dimension disagrees with the published value",
                name
            );
        }
    }

    /// The small-prime corpus identity solves, and reports the **same** published invariants.
    ///
    /// Two things at once. Zeebra reaches `GF(32003)` through `Zp32003`, a 32-bit Montgomery field
    /// rather than the 64-bit path the reference prime uses, so this is the only test that the
    /// adapter's new dispatch arm is wired at all. And the quotient dimension is an invariant of
    /// the *ideal's geometry*, which does not depend on the prime for these families — so a
    /// small-prime row disagreeing with the reference row's `D` would mean a generator, not a
    /// field, had changed.
    #[test]
    fn test_the_small_prime_rungs_solve_and_report_the_same_invariants() {
        for (name, published) in [("katsura-5", 16), ("katsura-6", 32), ("cyclic-5", 70)] {
            let built = instance(name, SMALL_PRIME_CHARACTERISTIC, GREVLEX).expect("builds");
            assert_eq!(built.field.characteristic, SMALL_PRIME_CHARACTERISTIC);
            let report = ZeebraSolver::new().run(&built, Task::ReducedGrobnerBasis, &limits());
            let answer = match &report.status {
                RunStatus::Complete(answer) => answer,
                other => panic!("{} over GF(32003) gave {:?}", name, other),
            };
            assert_eq!(
                answer.invariants.quotient_dimension,
                Some(published),
                "{} over GF(32003) disagrees with the published quotient dimension",
                name
            );
        }
    }

    /// The version probe reports this build, so a row is attributable to a commit.
    #[test]
    fn test_the_version_probe_reports_this_build() {
        let version = ZeebraSolver::new().probe_version().expect("in-process");
        assert_eq!(version.tool, "zeebra");
        assert_eq!(version.version, env!("CARGO_PKG_VERSION"));
    }

    /// The version probe records the selected `InterreductionRoute`, so two rows taken at one
    /// commit on either side of it are distinguishable in the board file.
    #[test]
    fn test_the_version_probe_records_the_interreduction_route() {
        use crate::grobner::{F4Options, InterreductionRoute};

        // Both flags named rather than inherited: `F4Options::default()` carries
        // `reduce_batch_rows: true` since #575, so a control taken from it would couple this
        // test's expected string to a default that is allowed to move.
        let probe = |route, reduce_batch_rows| {
            ZeebraSolver::new()
                .with_f4_options(F4Options {
                    interreduction: route,
                    reduce_batch_rows,
                    ..F4Options::default()
                })
                .probe_version()
                .expect("in-process")
        };

        for (route, expected) in [
            (InterreductionRoute::ByOrder, "by-order"),
            (InterreductionRoute::Division, "division"),
            (InterreductionRoute::Matrix, "matrix"),
        ] {
            assert_eq!(
                probe(route, false).build.as_deref(),
                Some(
                    format!(
                        "in-process, same build as the harness; \
                         interreduction={expected}, batch-rows=echelon"
                    )
                    .as_str()
                )
            );
        }

        // The second flag is named too, because the two interact: with batches left reduced the
        // autoreduce matrix the route chooses over is a few hundred columns rather than ~8 000.
        assert_eq!(
            probe(InterreductionRoute::Division, true).build.as_deref(),
            Some(
                "in-process, same build as the harness; \
                 interreduction=division, batch-rows=reduced"
            )
        );

        assert_ne!(
            probe(InterreductionRoute::Division, false).to_string(),
            probe(InterreductionRoute::Matrix, false).to_string()
        );
    }

    /// No memory figure unless the caller declares this process is doing one row.
    ///
    /// `VmHWM` is process-wide, so in a multi-row process it is a ceiling over all of them. The
    /// figure is withheld rather than approximated.
    #[test]
    fn test_no_memory_figure_unless_the_process_is_doing_one_row() {
        let katsura = instance("katsura-4", REFERENCE_CHARACTERISTIC, GREVLEX).expect("builds");

        let mut shared = ZeebraSolver::new();
        let report = shared.run(&katsura, Task::ReducedGrobnerBasis, &limits());
        assert_eq!(report.measurement.peak_rss_bytes, None);
        assert_eq!(report.measurement.rss_basis, RssBasis::Unavailable);

        let mut sole = ZeebraSolver::new().measuring_one_row();
        let report = sole.run(&katsura, Task::ReducedGrobnerBasis, &limits());
        if cfg!(target_os = "linux") {
            assert!(report.measurement.peak_rss_bytes.is_some());
            assert_eq!(report.measurement.rss_basis, RssBasis::SelfHighWater);
        }
    }

    /// A conversion row charges the basis and the conversion as separate stages.
    ///
    /// The report's stage attribution depends on this split: a total is not actionable.
    #[test]
    fn test_a_conversion_row_splits_basis_from_conversion() {
        let katsura = instance("katsura-4", REFERENCE_CHARACTERISTIC, GREVLEX).expect("builds");
        let mut solver = ZeebraSolver::new();
        let report = solver.run(&katsura, Task::LexBasisByConversion, &limits());
        assert!(
            matches!(report.status, RunStatus::Complete(_)),
            "{:?}",
            report.status
        );

        let names: Vec<&str> = report
            .measurement
            .stages
            .iter()
            .map(|(name, _)| name.as_str())
            .collect();
        assert!(names.contains(&"basis"), "{:?}", names);
        assert!(names.contains(&"conversion"), "{:?}", names);
        assert!(
            names
                .iter()
                .any(|n| n.starts_with("conversion: dependence")),
            "the lex walk is the stage the report attributes the gap to: {:?}",
            names
        );
    }

    /// A basis row reports its quotient dimension even where a *conversion* would be refused.
    ///
    /// The defect this guards cost a real row its invariant. `mq-planted-14`'s basis finishes in
    /// 0.28 ms, but its quotient is 16 384-dimensional, and the dimension was being obtained by
    /// running a full `fglm_to_lex` — which trips the 4 096 conversion cap. So the row went out
    /// with **no dimension at all**, against a Singular row that reported 16 384, and
    /// `Invariants::agree` compares only fields both sides carry: nothing shared is not agreement,
    /// so the row was silently unverified while sitting in a table of rows that agreed.
    ///
    /// A dimension needs the standard monomials enumerated and nothing else, so it gets its own
    /// far higher ceiling and a basis row's verifiability no longer depends on a conversion budget.
    #[test]
    fn test_a_basis_row_reports_its_dimension_above_the_conversion_cap() {
        let built = instance("mq-planted-14", REFERENCE_CHARACTERISTIC, GREVLEX).expect("builds");
        let mut solver = ZeebraSolver::new();
        let report = solver.run(&built, Task::ReducedGrobnerBasis, &limits());

        let answer = match &report.status {
            RunStatus::Complete(answer) => answer,
            other => panic!("the basis itself succeeds: {:?}", other),
        };
        assert_eq!(
            answer.invariants.quotient_dimension,
            Some(16_384),
            "the basis row must carry the dimension Singular also reports"
        );

        // And the conversion on the same rung is still refused, by the conversion's own budget.
        let conversion = solver.run(&built, Task::LexBasisByConversion, &limits());
        assert!(
            matches!(conversion.status, RunStatus::StoppedByLimit(_)),
            "{:?}",
            conversion.status
        );
    }

    /// An FGLM budget is a **limit stop**, not a failure, so no ratio can use its time.
    ///
    /// Found on the first corpus sweep that reached a rung over the default cap:
    /// `mq-planted-14` has a 16 384-dimensional quotient against `FglmOptions`'s default of 4 096,
    /// and the row was being recorded as `Failed` — putting a declared budget in the bucket
    /// reserved for an engine that broke. `StoppedByLimit` is the honest outcome and, unlike
    /// `Failed`, it is also what keeps the row out of a speedup.
    #[test]
    fn test_an_fglm_budget_is_a_limit_stop_and_not_a_failure() {
        let built = instance("mq-planted-14", REFERENCE_CHARACTERISTIC, GREVLEX).expect("builds");
        let mut solver = ZeebraSolver::new();
        let report = solver.run(&built, Task::LexBasisByConversion, &limits());

        match &report.status {
            RunStatus::StoppedByLimit(stop) => {
                let detail = stop.to_string();
                assert!(detail.contains("dimension"), "{}", detail);
            }
            other => panic!(
                "a declared quotient-dimension budget must be a limit stop, got {:?}",
                other
            ),
        }
        assert!(
            !report.status.is_timing_evidence(),
            "a limit stop is a lower bound, never a measurement"
        );
    }

    /// Raising the dimension cap surfaces the **memory** budget, by name.
    ///
    /// This is what the test above's stop being a *cap* actually looks like, and #623 is why it is
    /// written this way rather than asserting the rung completes.
    ///
    /// `max_quotient_dimension` is documented as "not the limit that bounds memory ... the
    /// pre-enumeration tripwire", with `max_span_entries` and `max_span_bytes` as the real bound —
    /// see [`RECOMMENDED_MAX_QUOTIENT_DIMENSION`]. `mq-planted-14`'s quotient dimension is 16 384,
    /// and `largest_quotient_dimension_within(RECOMMENDED_MAX_SPAN_ENTRIES)` is **3 162**, so the
    /// rung is 5.2x outside the memory envelope *by design*. **No setting of the tripwire can admit
    /// it**; only disabling the memory budget could, and that `D^2 + D(D+1)` is 536 887 296 cells
    /// — about 4 GB over a 64-bit prime field.
    ///
    /// So the property worth pinning is the one the two-layer design promises and a caller hitting
    /// these caps depends on: raise the tripwire and the *next* cap announces itself, naming
    /// itself, still as a limit stop rather than a failure. The original test asserted completion
    /// instead, which was true only in the window between the corpus growing this rung and
    /// `b2283f1e` adding the dependence-test budget — it has failed ever since, invisibly, because
    /// it was `#[ignore]`d.
    ///
    /// **Not ignored any more.** It was marked "needs minutes and gigabytes", which described the
    /// conversion it no longer reaches: the span budget is checked from `D` before anything is
    /// allocated, so this runs in about 0.2 s. An ignored test is not evidence, which was #623's
    /// complaint; this one now runs in CI.
    #[test]
    fn test_raising_the_dimension_cap_surfaces_the_memory_budget() {
        use crate::grobner::fglm::{
            largest_quotient_dimension_within, RECOMMENDED_MAX_SPAN_ENTRIES,
        };

        // The premise, asserted rather than asserted-in-prose: this rung is outside the memory
        // envelope whatever the tripwire says, so a test expecting completion could not pass.
        const DIMENSION: usize = 16_384;
        let admissible = largest_quotient_dimension_within(RECOMMENDED_MAX_SPAN_ENTRIES);
        assert!(
            DIMENSION > admissible,
            "this rung's dimension {} is within the span budget's {}, so the test no longer \
             describes the situation it was written for",
            DIMENSION,
            admissible
        );

        let built = instance("mq-planted-14", REFERENCE_CHARACTERISTIC, GREVLEX).expect("builds");
        let mut solver = ZeebraSolver::new().with_fglm_options(crate::grobner::FglmOptions {
            max_quotient_dimension: 32_768,
            ..crate::grobner::FglmOptions::default()
        });
        let report = solver.run(&built, Task::LexBasisByConversion, &limits());

        match &report.status {
            RunStatus::StoppedByLimit(stop) => {
                let detail = stop.to_string();
                // The span budget, not the dimension cap the test above gets.
                assert!(
                    detail.contains("dependence test"),
                    "the stop should now name the dependence-test budget: {}",
                    detail
                );
                // The cell count is `D^2 + D(D+1)`, which is what makes this a fact about the
                // dimension rather than a number to be re-copied when something drifts.
                let cells = DIMENSION * DIMENSION + DIMENSION * (DIMENSION + 1);
                assert!(
                    detail.contains(&cells.to_string()),
                    "the stop should carry the {} cells this dimension needs: {}",
                    cells,
                    detail
                );
                assert!(
                    detail.contains(&RECOMMENDED_MAX_SPAN_ENTRIES.to_string()),
                    "the stop should carry the limit it crossed: {}",
                    detail
                );
            }
            other => panic!(
                "raising the dimension cap should surface the next cap as a limit stop, got {:?}",
                other
            ),
        }
        assert!(
            !report.status.is_timing_evidence(),
            "a limit stop is a lower bound, never a measurement"
        );
    }

    /// A positive-dimensional ideal cannot be converted, and that is a fact about the ideal.
    ///
    /// `cyclic-4` is the corpus's positive-dimensional rung. The outcome is `Unsupported`, not a
    /// failure, and carries no time — so it cannot enter a ratio.
    #[test]
    fn test_conversion_of_a_positive_dimensional_ideal_is_unsupported() {
        let cyclic = instance("cyclic-4", REFERENCE_CHARACTERISTIC, GREVLEX).expect("builds");
        let mut solver = ZeebraSolver::new();
        let report = solver.run(&cyclic, Task::LexBasisByConversion, &limits());
        assert!(
            matches!(
                report.status,
                RunStatus::Unsupported(Unsupported::PositiveDimensional {
                    task: Task::LexBasisByConversion
                })
            ),
            "{:?}",
            report.status
        );
        assert!(!report.status.is_timing_evidence());
    }

    /// An uncompiled dimension is refused as an **adapter** limit, naming itself as one.
    ///
    /// A board row reading "unsupported" against Zeebra must not be read as a limit of the engine,
    /// which solves plenty of shapes this file does not instantiate.
    #[test]
    fn test_an_uncompiled_dimension_names_itself_an_adapter_limit() {
        let mut wide = instance("katsura-4", REFERENCE_CHARACTERISTIC, GREVLEX).expect("builds");
        // The narrowest width this adapter does **not** compile, found rather than written: the
        // compiled set grows with the ladder, and a literal here went stale the moment #424 added
        // the widths 8 to 11.
        let uncompiled = (1..)
            .find(|width| !compiled_dimensions(REFERENCE_CHARACTERISTIC).contains(width))
            .expect("some width is uncompiled");
        wide.variables = (1..=uncompiled).map(|i| format!("x{}", i)).collect();

        match ZeebraSolver::new().supports(&wide, Task::ReducedGrobnerBasis) {
            Support::No(Unsupported::Representation { detail }) => {
                assert!(detail.contains("adapter"), "{}", detail);
                assert!(
                    detail.contains("not a limit of Zeebra's engine"),
                    "{}",
                    detail
                );
            }
            other => panic!("expected an adapter-limit refusal, got {:?}", other),
        }
    }

    /// Every corpus field compiles the dimensions its own families need, and no others.
    ///
    /// Derived from the ladder rather than written out, so a rung added at a new width fails here
    /// until the adapter compiles it — which is the coupling the hard-coded list used to hide.
    /// KoalaBear and `2^64 - 353` carry no ladder rung; their widths are the ones callers' systems
    /// have been measured at.
    #[test]
    fn test_each_field_compiles_the_dimensions_its_families_need() {
        use std::collections::BTreeSet;
        // Derived from the ladder rather than written out, so a rung added at a new width fails
        // here until the adapter compiles it — which is the coupling a hard-coded list hid.
        for characteristic in [REFERENCE_CHARACTERISTIC, SMALL_PRIME_CHARACTERISTIC] {
            let needed: BTreeSet<usize> = crate::grobner::external::corpus::ladder()
                .into_iter()
                .filter_map(|entry| instance(entry.name, characteristic, GREVLEX))
                .filter(|built| built.field.characteristic == characteristic)
                .map(|built| built.variable_count())
                .collect();
            let compiled: BTreeSet<usize> = compiled_dimensions(characteristic)
                .iter()
                .copied()
                .collect();
            assert_eq!(
                compiled, needed,
                "GF({}) compiles {:?} where the ladder needs {:?}",
                characteristic, compiled, needed
            );
        }
        // The alternate prime carries the same families, and is the only field at which an
        // eliminant comparison against msolve exists at all (#465).
        assert_eq!(
            compiled_dimensions(ALTERNATE_CHARACTERISTIC),
            compiled_dimensions(REFERENCE_CHARACTERISTIC)
        );
        // Every prime the corpus generates the const-generic families over has a compiled arm, so
        // a corpus prime added there cannot leave Zeebra as the one engine with no operand.
        for characteristic in crate::grobner::external::corpus::GENERATED_CHARACTERISTICS {
            assert!(
                !compiled_dimensions(characteristic).is_empty(),
                "GF({}) is a generated corpus prime with no compiled Zeebra arm",
                characteristic
            );
        }
        assert!(compiled_dimensions(65521).is_empty());
    }

    /// A wall-clock limit produces a limit stop, not a failure, and carries no publishable time.
    #[test]
    fn test_a_wall_clock_limit_is_a_limit_stop() {
        let katsura = instance("katsura-7", REFERENCE_CHARACTERISTIC, GREVLEX).expect("builds");
        let mut solver = ZeebraSolver::new().with_f4_options(crate::grobner::F4Options {
            // A budget nothing can meet, so the cooperative check fires on the first boundary.
            max_wall_time: Some(Duration::from_nanos(1)),
            ..crate::grobner::F4Options::default()
        });
        let report = solver.run(&katsura, Task::ReducedGrobnerBasis, &limits());
        assert!(
            matches!(report.status, RunStatus::StoppedByLimit(_)),
            "{:?}",
            report.status
        );
        assert!(
            !report.status.is_timing_evidence(),
            "a limit stop is a lower bound, never a measurement"
        );
    }
}
